# TypeScript Documentation Best Practices

Creating comprehensive documentation for your TypeScript projects follows systematic approaches that ensure maintainability and discoverability.

## Organized Documentation Strategy

Structure documentation thematically rather than alphabetically. Group related concepts together based on functionality or use cases. This mirrors how developers typically search - they're looking for solutions to specific problems rather than alphabetically.

### Core Documentation Structure

Start with high-level architecture overviews that explain your code's philosophy and design decisions. This helps new team members understand the "why" behind implementation choices before diving into "how."

#### API Reference Format

```typescript
/**
 * Adds two numbers
 * @param a - First number
 * @param b - Second number
 * @returns Sum of a and b
 */
const add = (a: number, b: number): number => {
  return a + b;
}

type User = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  name?: {
    first: string;
    last: string;
  };
}

interface UserService {
  fetchUser(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
  updateUserProfile(user: Partial<User>): Promise<void>;
}

class UserInitialize implements OnDestroy {
  constructor(private dataService: DataService) {}
}

function initializeApp(): void {
  // initialize logic
}
```

### Documentation Standards

Follow consistent formatting throughout your codebase. Use JSDoc-style comments for functions and methods, inline comments for complex logic, and separate documentation files for major libraries or modules.

Document edge cases explicitly - what happens when inputs are null? When users have insufficient privileges? When network calls fail?

#### Version Control

Keep documentation in your version control system. It evolves alongside your code, ensuring it stays current.
